## Bundled — `rules/vendor/`

**`Aaronontheweb/dotnet-cursor-rules`** — MIT
<https://github.com/Aaronontheweb/dotnet-cursor-rules>

The eight `aaron-*.md` files under `rules/vendor/` are adapted from that project.
They cover C# coding style, xUnit testing, .NET build systems, SDK/solution
management, NuGet dependency management, code signing, and dotnet tool
consumption/publishing.

These files are kept in `rules/vendor/` — separate from the authored rules in
`rules/pipeline/` — so the boundary between vendored and original content stays
visible.

**Adapted, not vendored verbatim, and not pinned to an upstream revision.** The
files reached this project through an intermediate copy, and the exact upstream
commit was not recorded; they have also been modified here — a `paths:` key was
added alongside `globs:` so Claude Code loads them by glob the way Cursor does
(see `rules/vendor/README.md`).

A future re-sync therefore means a manual diff against current upstream, not a
fast-forward. The MIT licence requires attribution and the licence text, both of
which this file carries; a commit hash is not part of that obligation, and an
invented one would be worse than none.

## Bundled — `skills/`

Each adapted skill also carries its attribution inline, in the file itself.

**`codewithmukesh/dotnet-claude-kit`** — MIT
<https://github.com/codewithmukesh/dotnet-claude-kit>

Seven skills are adapted from that project: `verify`, `testing`, `scaffold`,
`modern-csharp`, `resilience`, `opentelemetry`, and `convention-learner`.

In every case the upstream Roslyn MCP calls have been replaced with standard
`dotnet` tooling plus this harness's gate scripts, so no skill here depends on an
MCP server or on that plugin being installed. Stack-specific examples were
generalised, since this harness does not assume a database or API style.

**`addyosmani/agent-skills`** — MIT
<https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills>

`ship-review`'s parallel fan-out structure is inspired by that project's `/ship`
skill, re-mapped to this harness's pipeline stages and its own agents.
`using-agent-skills` and `test-engineer` are likewise adapted from their
counterparts there.

**`davila7/claude-code-templates`** — MIT
<https://github.com/davila7/claude-code-templates>

`k6-load-testing` is adapted from the community skill of the same name.

## Runtime dependency — not bundled

**GitHub Spec Kit** — MIT
<https://github.com/github/spec-kit>

The harness chains into Spec Kit rather than vendoring it. `speckit/extensions.yml`
registers this harness's pipeline stages as Spec Kit extension hooks. Spec Kit is
installed separately:

```bash
uv tool install specify-cli --from git+https://github.com/github/spec-kit.git@v0.8.14
```

Pinned version: `0.8.14`.

## Tooling invoked by the gates — not bundled

These are resolved as local `dotnet` tools or NuGet packages by the consuming
repository; the harness only wires and invokes them.

| Tool | License | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.NetAnalyzers` | MIT | Roslyn analyzer + CA1502 complexity gate |
| `Microsoft.VisualStudio.Threading.Analyzers` | MIT | Async/threading diagnostics |
| `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.BannedApiAnalyzers` | MIT | `BannedSymbols.txt` enforcement |
| `dotnet-stryker` | Apache-2.0 | Mutation testing gate |
| `JetBrains.ReSharper.GlobalTools` | Free to use (JetBrains EULA) | InspectCode duplication/inspection gate |
| `FsCheck` | BSD-3-Clause | Property-test gate |

Verify each tool's current license terms before redistributing anything that
embeds them. The harness itself embeds none of them.
